6re|n|An inlet of the sea, usually smaller than a gulf, but of the same general character. ; A small body of water set off from the main body; as a compartment containing water for a wheel; the portion of a canal just outside of the gates of a lock, etc. ; A recess or indentation shaped like a bay. ; A principal compartment of the walls, roof, or other part of a building, or of the whole building, as marked off by the buttresses, vaulting, mullions of a window, etc.; one of the main divisions of any structure, as the part of a bridge between two piers. ; A compartment in a barn, for depositing hay, or grain in the stalks. ; A kind of mahogany obtained from Campeachy Bay. ; A berry, particularly of the laurel. ; The laurel tree (Laurus nobilis). Hence, in the plural, an honorary garland or crown bestowed as a prize for victory or excellence, anciently made or consisting of branches of the laurel. ; A tract covered with bay trees. ; A bank or dam to keep back water.	N:4ix,63,1~4ix,63,1
6re|a|Reddish brown; of the color of a chestnut; -- applied to the color of horses.	j:4ix,63,1~4ix,63,1
